.TH RCAL 1 "September 2026" "rcal 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rcal \- CLI calendar tool with CalDAV synchronization
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rcal
.RI [ COMMAND ] [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rcal
is a command-line calendar tool. It keeps an offline SQLite cache of your
events, displays day, week and month views, imports iCal (.ics) files, and
synchronizes bidirectionally with a CalDAV server (for example Radicale,
DAViCal, or Nextcloud Calendar). Read-only online ICS calendar feeds can be
pulled in with
.B subscribe
and are refreshed during
.BR sync .
.PP
Use
.B sync
for calendar work; first run
.B add-account
to create a config file (or write one by hand; see CONFIGURATION).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help information.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version information.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B today
Show today's events. The alias
.B day
is accepted.
.TP
.RS
.IP "\fB\-d, \-\-date\fR \fIDATE\fR"
Date to show (\fIYYYY\-MM\-DD\fR); defaults to today.
.IP "\fB\-\-details\fR"
Show all details of each event.
.IP "\fB\-\-next\fR"
Advance one day per occurrence; repeatable (so \fB\-\-next \-\-next\fR shows
the day after tomorrow).
.RE
.TP
.B week
Show an overview of the week containing the given date, or the current week.
.TP
.RS
.IP "\fB\-d, \-\-date\fR \fIDATE\fR"
Start date (\fIYYYY\-MM\-DD\fR); defaults to Monday of the current week.
.IP "\fB\-\-next\fR"
Advance one week per occurrence; repeatable.
.IP "\fB\-\-agenda\fR"
Append a compact day-by-day listing (one time and summary line per event).
.IP "\fB\-\-details\fR"
Append a day-by-day listing with every event expanded to all its details.
.RE
.TP
.B month
Show an overview of the given month, or the current month.
.TP
.RS
.IP "\fB\-m, \-\-month\fR \fIMONTH\fR"
Month (\fIYYYY\-MM\fR); defaults to the current month.
.IP "\fB\-\-next\fR"
Advance one month per occurrence; repeatable.
.IP "\fB\-\-agenda\fR"
Append a compact day-by-day listing (one time and summary line per event).
.IP "\fB\-\-details\fR"
Append a day-by-day listing with every event expanded to all its details.
.RE
.TP
.B show
Show events for a specific date. To inspect a single event in full detail,
append a start time.
.TP
.RS
.IP "\fBDATE\fR"
Date to show (\fIYYYY\-MM\-DD\fR, or \fIYYYY\-MM\-DD@HH:MM\fR to render the
details of the single event active at that time).
.IP "\fB\-\-details\fR"
Show all details of each event on the date.
.RE
.TP
.B import
Import an iCal (.ics) file. Interactively previews each event before adding;
a report is shown after the import.
.TP
.RS
.IP "\fBFILE\fR"
Path to the .ics file.
.IP "\fB\-\-add\fR"
Add all events without interactive prompts.
.IP "\fB\-\-dry-run\fR"
Preview only; do not modify the database.
.RE
.TP
.B sync
Synchronize with every CalDAV server configured in the config file. For each
account under
.B [[servers]]
it discovers calendars, pulls changes (adds, updates, deletions), and pushes
locally created events with an HTTP PUT; it then refreshes any ICS
subscriptions sequentially. Prints per-calendar counts of added, updated,
unchanged, deleted and pushed events.
.TP
.B new
Create a new event. When run interactively (a TTY), missing values are
prompted for. When run non-interactively, \fB\-\-title\fR is required and
defaults are used for everything else.
.TP
.RS
.IP "\fB\-t, \-\-title\fR \fITITLE\fR"
Event title.
.IP "\fB\-d, \-\-date\fR \fIDATE\fR"
Event date (\fIYYYY\-MM\-DD\fR); defaults to today.
.IP "\fB\-\-time\fR \fITIME\fR"
Start time (24h \fIHH:MM\fR); defaults to 09:00.
.IP "\fB\-D, \-\-duration\fR \fIMINUTES\fR"
Duration in minutes; defaults to 60.
.IP "\fB\-a, \-\-all-day\fR"
Create an all-day event.
.IP "\fB\-l, \-\-location\fR \fILOCATION\fR"
Event location.
.IP "\fB\-\-description\fR \fIDESCRIPTION\fR"
Event description.
.IP "\fB\-c, \-\-calendar\fR \fICALENDAR\fR"
Target calendar (name or URL as shown by the \fBcalendars\fR command).
.RE
.TP
.B search
Search events by text. Matches the summary, description and location
(case-insensitive).
.TP
.RS
.IP "\fBQUERY\fR"
Search query.
.IP "\fB\-\-from\fR \fIDATE\fR"
Lower bound of the date range (\fIYYYY\-MM\-DD\fR).
.IP "\fB\-\-to\fR \fIDATE\fR"
Upper bound of the date range (\fIYYYY\-MM\-DD\fR).
.RE
.TP
.B delete
Delete an event locally and, for CalDAV-synced events, on the server.
CalDAV events are removed from the server first; events cached from an ICS
subscription are only removed locally (the feed re-adds them on the next
refresh).
.TP
.RS
.IP "\fBTARGET\fR"
The event to delete: \fIYYYY\-MM\-DD@HH:MM\fR for the event starting at that
time on that date, or just \fIYYYY\-MM\-DD\fR when exactly one event starts
that day.
.IP "\fB\-c, \-\-calendar\fR \fICALENDAR\fR"
Restrict the match to one calendar (name, URL, or the literal
.BR local ).
.IP "\fB\-f, \-\-force\fR"
Delete without confirmation.
.RE
.TP
.B calendars
List available calendars with their config color swatches and event counts.
.TP
.B subscribe
Subscribe to an online ICS calendar feed and cache it locally, so its events
appear in the views. No CalDAV account is required.
.TP
.RS
.IP "\fBURL\fR"
URL of the .ics feed.
.IP "\fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR"
Display name for the subscription (defaults to the feed host).
.RE
.TP
.B add-account
Add a CalDAV account to the config file at the default location. When run
interactively (a TTY), the server URL, username and optional password command
are prompted for. When run non-interactively,
.B \-\-url
and
.B \-\-username
are required. The account is appended to any existing
.B [[servers]]
entries; a duplicate URL is rejected. When no config exists yet, one is
created.
.TP
.RS
.IP "\fB\-u, \-\-url\fR \fIURL\fR"
CalDAV server URL.
.IP "\fB\-\-username\fR \fIUSERNAME\fR"
CalDAV username.
.IP "\fB\-\-password-command\fR \fICOMMAND\fR"
Shell command whose stdout is the password.
.IP "\fB\-f, \-\-force\fR"
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.RE
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B rcal
reads a TOML configuration file. By default it looks in
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rcal/config.toml
or (when that variable is unset)
.IR ~/.config/rcal/config.toml .
Create it with
.B "rcal add-account"
or
.B "rcal subscribe"
(see COMMANDS) or by hand. At least one
.B [[servers]]
entry or
.B [[subscriptions]]
entry is needed for a useful setup; several accounts and feeds may be
configured, and a legacy single
.B [server]
table is still accepted.
.PP
.nf
[[servers]]
url = "https://dav.example.com/"
username = "alice"
password_command = "pass show caldav/alice"

[display]
default_view = "today"
time_format = "24h"
color_scheme = "auto"
accent_color = "#d970ff"

[[calendar_colors]]
name = "Work"
color = "#e6194b"

[[calendar_colors]]
name = "Holidays"
color = "#42d4f4"

[[subscriptions]]
name = "Holidays"
url = "https://example.com/holidays.ics"
.fi
.PP
The
.B display
table and the
.B servers ,
.B subscriptions
and
.B calendar_colors
lists are optional and their values fall back to the defaults shown above.
.B accent_color
is the \fI#RRGGBB\fR terminal color used to accent event summaries; events
belonging to a calendar with a
.B [[calendar_colors]] 
entry use that calendar's color instead. Calendar colors are read from this
config file at display time and are never stored in the database.
.PP
To set a specific color for a calendar, add a
.B [[calendar_colors]]
entry for its display name (as shown by the \fBcalendars\fR command) and an
\fI#RRGGBB\fR color:
.nf
[[calendar_colors]]
name = "Work"
color = "#e6194b"
.fi
.PP
Removing the entry falls back to
.BR accent_color .
Each subscribed feed appears as its own entry under
.BR subscriptions .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RCAL_PASSWORD
Password used for CalDAV authentication, used when
.B password_command
is not configured. The password is resolved in this order: the
.I password_command
from the config file, then the \fBRCAL_PASSWORD\fR environment variable, then
an interactive prompt. Passwords are never stored in the database.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Overrides the base directory for the config file.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Overrides the base directory for the database.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/rcal/config.toml
Configuration file (see CONFIGURATION).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/rcal/rcal.db
SQLite database holding the offline event cache and sync metadata
(etags, calendar associations).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Create a config file interactively, or with flags:
.nf
$ rcal add-account
$ rcal add-account -u https://dav.example.com/ --username alice \\
           --password-command "pass show caldav/alice"
.fi
.PP
Show today's events:
.nf
$ rcal today
.fi
.PP
Show the events of March 1, 2026 (or the single event active at 10:30):
.nf
$ rcal show 2026-03-01
$ rcal show 2026-03-01@10:30
.fi
.PP
Preview an iCal file without importing and then import everything:
.nf
$ rcal import schedule.ics --dry-run
$ rcal import schedule.ics --add
.fi
.PP
Create a one-hour meeting on a specific calendar:
.nf
$ rcal new --title "Design review" --date 2026-03-02 --time 14:00 \\
           --location "Room 3" --calendar "http://dav.example.com/alice/work/"
.fi
.PP
Subscribe to a read-only ICS feed and pull in every configured account:
.nf
$ rcal subscribe --name Holidays https://example.com/holidays.ics
$ rcal sync
.fi
.PP
Delete an event and synchronize calendars:
.nf
$ rcal delete 2026-03-02@14:00
$ rcal calendars
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exit status is \fB0\fR on success and \fB1\fR on error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR radicale (5),
.br
RFC 5545 (Internet Calendaring and Scheduling Core Object Specification)